@@ -140,3 +140,18 @@ On Windows, temporary executables, compiler scratch files, disposable journals a
 Run `npm test` from `unreal-auto-setup/`, or from the repository root run `node tools/run-tests.cjs tests/process-supervision.test.js tests/operation-ownership.test.js tests/runner.test.js`. The launcher creates an exclusive `suite-*` directory and passes `TEMP`, `TMP`, `TMPDIR` and `UNREAL_AUTO_SETUP_TEST_TEMP` only to the Node test process and its children. It removes that owned directory when the suite exits, including test failure. `tests/testTemp.js` also isolates direct `node --test` invocations into `node-<pid>-*` directories and directs compiler-internal temporary output there. Each fixture still uses its own `mkdtemp` directory. Python packaging tests use `TemporaryDirectory` under the same dedicated root.
 
 `tools/phase3b2b/build-helper.ps1` scopes compiler temporary variables to its invocation and restores them in `finally`; the distributable helper binary remains beside its permanent source under `unreal-auto-setup/native/`. `node tools/phase3b2b/launch-integration.cjs` creates an `integration-*` scratch directory containing the isolated VS Code profile, driver, fixture executable and supervised-operation journal. Integration directories are retained on uncertainty/failure; remove only after the owned hosts/processes are confirmed stopped and unresolved evidence has been archived. A killed runner cannot execute cleanup; inspect retained directories rather than deleting unknown active work. The dedicated directory must be writable; no fallback to Windows Temp is used on Windows.
+
+## Phase 4 Testing, Debugger Lifecycle and Release Engineering
+
+### C++ Creation Tools & Snippets (Phase 4A)
+The C++ template registry (`classTemplates.js`) supports 17 Unreal type templates, including header-only structs (`F`) and enums (`E`), and class pairs (Character, Pawn, Controller, Game Mode, Function Library, Data Asset, UObject, Actor, Components, Subsystems, Interfaces). Each generated class is placed in its own dedicated subfolder. Four native C++ snippets (`ustruct`, `uenum`, `uproperty`, `ufunction`) are contributed via `snippets/unreal.json`.
+
+### Debugger Lifecycle (Phase 4B)
+The debugger integration (`debugger.js`) attaches to Unreal Editor via `cppvsdbg`. It enforces single-session attachment per Editor PID, rejecting duplicate attempts before dispatching to VS Code debug APIs. `detach()` cleans up debug sessions via `vscode.debug.stopDebugging()` without terminating the underlying Editor process. Reattachment to the same Editor instance is fully supported.
+
+### Testing Infrastructure & Release Verification (Phase 4C)
+- **Extension Host Testing**: `node tools/run-extension-tests.cjs` launches the real VS Code Extension Development Host with an isolated scratch profile and workspace, exercising 20 editor integration scenarios via `tests/host-runner-suite.js`.
+- **Reliable Engine Validation**: `tests/real-unreal-validation.js` guarantees explicit stage tracking (`Passed`, `Failed`, `Skipped`), timeout-bounded child execution, and nonzero exit codes on failure. Tested via synthetic fault injection in `tests/engine-validation-failures.test.js`.
+- **Release Checks**: `node tools/validate-release.cjs` executes 12 automated checks covering syntax, packaging integrity, public commands/settings, supervisor binary integrity, documentation mirrors, links, and test suites. Optional flags: `--include-host` and `--include-engine`.
+- **Windows CI**: Checked-in `.github/workflows/windows-ci.yml` organizes fast checks, native helper compilation, Extension Host validation, and opt-in real engine integration on Windows runners.
+
